segments.translation:
With the [refrain]{dhruvapada} he speaks, illuminating the fourth [joy]{ananda}. "For the sake of [dharma]{dharma}": a dharma is so called because it holds its own characteristic; it is a transformation of the elements, such as a pot, a cloth, a pillar or a jar. In its [true nature]{svarupa} it has no form, through the [non-apprehension]{anupalambha} that follows the analysis taught in the chapter on [reality]{tattva} of the glorious [Heruka Tantra]{tx-heruka-tantra}. The [siddha]{siddha} master [Cāṭila]{p-catila} builds, through the [guru's lineage]{guru-sampradaya}, "a [bridge]{bridge}", that is, the unity of the [conventional]{samvrti} and the [ultimate]{paramartha}. And so [Sarahapāda]{p-saraha} says:
Whoever joins [emptiness]{sunyata} and [compassion]{karuna}
stays neither in [existence]{bhava} nor in [nirvāṇa]{nirvana};
but whoever meditates on compassion alone
does not gain [liberation]{moksa} in a thousand births.
By this means of the siddha master, those [yogis]{yogi} who are eager for liberation also reach the far shore of the ocean of [saṃsāra]{samsara} without fail.
